                <pb facs="0653" n="563" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg563"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.75 National Telephone Company/35:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain (Laughlin) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-11-18T15:00:00+00:00">November 18, 1932—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>86. Bill of December 10, 1931 for annulation of telephone contract has been
                    resuscitated. Company has been cited to appear before Communications Committee
                    of Cortes this afternoon to show cause why “illegal” contract should not be
                    abrogated. Interpellations directed against company are likewise expected. Azaña
                    does not appear to be antagonistic but he has made it clear to Rock that
                    position of Government is too delicate to enable him at present to afford much
                    parliamentary protection. The outlook for the company is serious.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Laughlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
